Renal Artery Stenosis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Refractory severe hypertension, Abdominal bruit, Flash pulmonary oedema, Renal decline post-ACE inhibitor

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-039
Reason for Visit: Sudden, terrifying onset of severe shortness of breath at rest, accompanied by an explosive headache and critically elevated blood pressure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden severe respiratory distress, frothy sputum, and a massive spike in blood pressure. Urgent after-hours assessment was absolutely critical to identify and manage flash pulmonary oedema and hypertensive crisis, conditions that pose an immediate, imminent threat of fatal cardiopulmonary collapse if left unassessed until regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I suddenly cannot catch my breath when I lie down, my chest feels like it is drowning, and my head is pounding relentlessly.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an abrupt exacerbation of severe, difficult-to-control hypertension over the past month. Their blood pressure has been refractory, requiring three antihypertensive medications, including a diuretic. Crucially, they report an unexplained acute worsening of their renal function blood tests immediately following the recent initiation of an ACE inhibitor by their regular doctor. Tonight, they experienced a terrifying episode of flash pulmonary oedema, presenting as sudden, severe respiratory distress and orthopnoea. They have a known history of generalized atherosclerosis and coronary artery disease.
Functional Status: Acutely distressed, unable to speak in full sentences, forced to sit bolt upright to breathe.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of respiratory failure and hypoxic cardiac arrest; spouse is in acute panic.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 225/120 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.5C | RR: 28 | SpO2: 89% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, pale, cyanotic at the lips, utilizing accessory muscles of respiration, and exhibiting profound acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, critically severe hypertension. A distinct, loud abdominal bruit is audible on auscultation just superior and lateral to the umbilicus. Respiratory: Bilateral coarse crackles and wheezes audible throughout all lung fields, consistent with severe acute pulmonary oedema. Extremities: Cool, clammy peripheries with delayed capillary refill. Expected investigation results upon hospital admission would include elevated peak systolic velocity on renal artery Doppler and focal stenosis on CT angiography.
Environmental Observations: Patient is slumped forward over a table to maximize respiratory effort.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently commenced on Perindopril (ACE inhibitor), which has precipitated this acute renovascular crisis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Renal Artery Stenosis - Status: Exacerbated (Hypertensive Crisis with Flash Pulmonary Oedema)
Differentials:

Primary Essential Hypertension - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of a loud abdominal bruit, the sudden flash pulmonary oedema, and the specific history of renal decline immediately following ACE inhibitor initiation.

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the extreme hypertensive crisis, the presence of coarse crackles (fluid) rather than pure expiratory wheeze, and the lack of response to typical bronchodilators.

Anxiety/Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to the objective presence of severe hypoxia, critical hypertension, and florid pulmonary crackles indicating true organic failure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-Threatening Emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient is in extremis, suffering from a catastrophic hypertensive crisis and flash pulmonary oedema directly driven by severe, underlying renal artery stenosis. The recent initiation of an ACE inhibitor critically reduced perfusion pressure to the stenotic kidney, triggering massive, uninhibited renin release and resultant explosive volume and pressure overload. The audible abdominal bruit confirms the renovascular pathology. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was the only way to recognize this acute cardiopulmonary collapse, immediately administer life-saving vasodilators, and orchestrate priority emergency transport. Delaying this intervention would have resulted in certain fatal hypoxic respiratory failure.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered sublingual Glyceryl Trinitrate (GTN) 400mcg spray immediately, repeating once after 5 minutes, to rapidly drop preload and systemic vascular resistance. Applied high-flow oxygen at 15L/min via non-rebreather mask. Arranged immediate priority ambulance transfer to the nearest Emergency Department for continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) and intravenous antihypertensives. Instructed the absolute, immediate cessation of the ACE inhibitor (Perindopril).
Positive Behavioral Change: No behavioral interventions are appropriate in this acute, life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergency; immediate mechanical and pharmacological stabilization is the sole priority.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Direct, urgent medical handover provided to the responding paramedics and the receiving Emergency Department physician.
DME/Safety (Optional): None, patient requires immediate intensive care admission.
Follow-up: General practice follow-up is deferred entirely to the hospital cardiology and renal teams.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatch has been prioritized as a Code 1 emergency. The patient must remain sitting strictly upright with their legs dangling over the side of the bed or chair to maximize venous pooling and reduce fluid return to the drowning lungs.

